---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';

const issuesUrl = `${SITE.repository}/issues`;
const githubPrivacyStatement = 'https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement';
const githubCookies = 'https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-cookies';
const githubPagesOverview = 'https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/what-is-github-pages';
---

<BaseLayout title="Privacy" description="What the CyberPulse Intelligence website collects, which services it contacts, and how to ask a question." path="/privacy/">
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Privacy"
    title="Privacy notice"
    description="A plain description of what this website does with visitor requests, based on how the site is built."
  />

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 pb-24 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="prose-article max-w-[68ch]">
      <p class="text-sm text-mist-500">Last reviewed: <time datetime="2026-11-27">27 November 2026</time>.</p>

      <h2>What this site is</h2>
      <p>
        CyberPulse Intelligence is a static website. Its pages are generated in advance and served from GitHub Pages.
        In the current code, the site has no user accounts, no login, no comments and no server of its own that
        handles visitor data.
      </p>

      <h2>Information you give us</h2>
      <p>
        In the current code, the site asks visitors for nothing. It has no contact forms and no newsletter sign-up.
        The site's research search, when available, runs in your browser only. Nothing you type on the site is sent to a server in the
        current code.
      </p>

      <h2>Cookies and browser storage</h2>
      <p>
        In the current code, the site does not set cookies and does not write to browser local storage or session
        storage.
      </p>
      <p>
        GitHub's cookie information describes the cookies GitHub uses on its own websites. It does not say which
        cookies, if any, are set on GitHub Pages sites. Read it on
        <a href={githubCookies} rel="noopener" target="_blank">GitHub's cookies page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Analytics and tracking</h2>
      <p>
        In the current code, the site includes no analytics, advertising or tracking code, and it loads no
        third-party scripts. The scripts that run in your browser are part of the site's own build.
      </p>

      <h2>Requests to other services</h2>
      <p>
        When you open a page, your browser requests it from GitHub Pages. GitHub's documentation for GitHub Pages says
        that the visitor's IP address is logged and stored for security purposes, regardless of whether the visitor has
        signed into GitHub. See
        <a href={githubPagesOverview} rel="noopener" target="_blank">What is GitHub Pages</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        GitHub's general privacy statement describes automatic logging of website interactions, including the
        referring site, date and time of visit, pages viewed and links clicked. This notice does not confirm how that
        statement applies to GitHub Pages sites specifically. Read it on
        <a href={githubPrivacyStatement} rel="noopener" target="_blank">GitHub's general privacy statement</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fonts used on the site are served from this site. The site does not contact Google Fonts or any other
        font service.
      </p>
      <p>
        Links to references and to the source repository open the other site in a new tab. Those sites have their
        own privacy practices, which this notice does not cover.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions and corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about this notice, and corrections to published research, are handled through the
        <a href={issuesUrl} rel="noopener" target="_blank">issue tracker on GitHub</a>. No other contact address is
        published for this site.
      </p>
      <p>
        The issue tracker is hosted by GitHub and is public. Questions, comments and other information you submit there
        may be visible to anyone. Items you post there are shown under your GitHub account name. Do not include
        passwords, personal information, confidential information or security-sensitive details.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes to this notice</h2>
      <p>
        If the site's data collection or use changes, we will update this notice to reflect those changes.
      </p>

      <p class="text-sm text-mist-500">
        This page describes how the site works. It is not legal advice, and it does not state a legal entity or a
        legal basis for processing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
